{
  "rules": [
    "Mild positive bias overall: forecasts tend to run ~0.2–1.5 EUR above realized levels, especially in the mid-horizon (≈days 8–20). Apply a small downward offset to the whole path, larger for days 8–20 than days 1–7.",
    "Over-extrapolation of late-horizon rallies: when the model projects a strong upswing into the last third of the horizon (≈days 20–30), it often overshoots peaks by ~1–3 EUR. Damp late-horizon slope/returns (shrink projected gains) when the forecast implies accelerating upside.",
    "Insufficient mean reversion after local highs: forecasts keep prices elevated longer than realized following a run-up. If the last 10–15 days in history show a sharp rise, increase mean-reversion strength (pull forecasts back toward recent average) rather than extending the trend.",
    "Turning-point lag: around inflection points, the model is slow to reflect pullbacks (stays too high) and slightly underestimates subsequent rebounds. Use a regime switch: after a multi-day rise, cap further increases; after a multi-day drop, allow a quicker bounce but with capped magnitude.",
    "Horizon-dependent calibration: keep near-term (days 1–5) adjustments small (errors are modest), apply strongest damping/negative bias in days 10–25, and apply peak-capping in days 25–30."
  ]
}
